\section*{Critical honeycomb polygon diameter and length exponents}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The linked manuscripts present the following claims by OpenAI: Critical polygons of diameter at most $H$ are claimed to have squared-length mass at most $H^{2/3+o(1)}$, with quantitative two-mark and unequal-arc bounds; Critical unrooted polygon diameter tails are claimed to have exponents $-2$ and $-2/3$ without and with length weighting, leading to diameter exponent $3/4$ under large-length conditioning. Together they give a claimed affirmative answer in the matching scope.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Finite cylinder vacua yield an exact marked-pair identity. A denominator estimate tolerates unbalanced multiplicities, and coercive magnetic trial actions handle unequal walls; the rare central scale then gives the uniform polynomial one-arc bound.

Two cylinder pressures and angular color-sum asymptotics are passed to a radial series. Determinant normalization and a one-mark Hilbert--Schmidt plateau bound control tails and moments; unequal-scale two-mark estimates complete the length-conditioned consequences.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The source supplies the marked second length-moment upper bound and one-arc estimates, not both sharp diameter-tail sums A(r) and P(r). Critical unrooted polygon diameter tails are claimed to have exponents $-2$ and $-2/3$ without and with length weighting, leading to diameter exponent $3/4$ under large-length conditioning. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 237, the selected Lean statements give critical-strip mass and displacement exponents, bridge summability, and existence of pulled free energy. The $4/3$ bridge-length and $3/4$ spatial laws and the other polygon/chord exponents are outside these statements. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

